{"parentUuid":"1b888bbf-9ab7-44e1-884b-2f5a6692ef63","isSidechain":true,"promptId":"6fa20b73-a995-4ae4-b928-344e193a8ce4","agentId":"aec9dfe82ab5889ef","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01LKrHx8xHCRuetEMc55H9k5","type":"tool_result","content":"# Paper Summary\n\n**Title:** \"Practically Feasible Proof Logging for Pseudo-Boolean Optimization\"\n\n**Authors:** Wietze Koops, Daniel Le Berre, Magnus O. Myreen, Jakob Nordström, Andy Oertel, Yong Kiam Tan, Marc Vinyals\n\n**Is it about pseudo-Boolean optimization proof logging?**\nYes, directly. The work presents tooling for formally verified pseudo-Boolean optimization using proof logging techniques.\n\n**Industrial Usability Claims:**\nThe authors assert that their implementation demonstrates practical viability at scale. They claim: \"proof logging and checking performance in this much more expressive paradigm is now quite close to the level of SAT solving, and hence is clearly practically feasible.\"\n\nThe paper implements proof logging across advanced solver techniques including core-guided search and linear programming integration. By achieving performance comparable to established SAT solver certification methods, the authors position their work as moving pseudo-Boolean proof logging from theoretical exercises into genuinely deployable systems suitable for real-world combinatorial optimization problems."}]},"uuid":"3970a309-303f-48fd-a581-c40ab2d2a231","timestamp":"2026-05-09T23:52:03.518Z","sourceToolAssistantUUID":"1b888bbf-9ab7-44e1-884b-2f5a6692ef63","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/home/zhuran24/claude-pj/zmd","sessionId":"7db7f276-a2bf-4762-b9b8-bb35f8cf3fb9","version":"2.1.138","gitBranch":"master","slug":"squishy-zooming-sphinx"}
{"parentUuid":"786b5b31-537d-4b2f-96c9-0e5a7dc08ed8","isSidechain":true,"promptId":"6fa20b73-a995-4ae4-b928-344e193a8ce4","agentId":"aec9dfe82ab5889ef","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_016NjbKokoQUzZiue3bpSFds","type":"tool_result","content":"I appreciate your request, but I need to clarify what you're asking for. The webpage content you provided is about a 2005 Dagstuhl seminar paper by Jean-Marie Favre titled \"Foundations of Meta-Pyramids: Languages vs. Metamodels – Episode II: Story of Thotus the Baboon.\"\n\nHowever, you've concluded your request by asking for \"the title, authors, and abstract of LIPIcs CP 2025 paper #21,\" which is a different paper entirely not contained in the provided content.\n\n**Based solely on the webpage above, here's what I can provide:**\n\n**Title:** \"Foundations of Meta-Pyramids: Languages vs. Metamodels – Episode II: Story of Thotus the Baboon\"\n\n**Author:** Jean-Marie Favre\n\n**Abstract Summary:** The paper examines the four-layer metamodeling architecture within Model-Driven Engineering, questioning whether \"instanceOf\" terminology from object-oriented technology appropriately applies to meta-pyramids. The author extends a megamodel concept to address relationships between languages and metamodels, using an allegorical narrative to explore foundational principles in metamodeling.\n\nIf you need information about the LIPIcs CP 2025 paper #21, please provide that source material."}]},"uuid":"47dc3a88-a5bb-4ef0-b761-a8f0dfe80110","timestamp":"2026-05-09T23:52:03.824Z","sourceToolAssistantUUID":"786b5b31-537d-4b2f-96c9-0e5a7dc08ed8","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/home/zhuran24/claude-pj/zmd","sessionId":"7db7f276-a2bf-4762-b9b8-bb35f8cf3fb9","version":"2.1.138","gitBranch":"master","slug":"squishy-zooming-sphinx"}
{"parentUuid":"1fb092c1-ea26-4f58-85b0-ed9b5182b272","isSidechain":true,"promptId":"6fa20b73-a995-4ae4-b928-344e193a8ce4","agentId":"aec9dfe82ab5889ef","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01RGQsMcMSDdU8wEzZWfKJX5","type":"tool_result","content":"Web search results for query: \"\"Lübke\" \"Berg\" \"Core-Boosted\" \"Anytime Maximum Satisfiability\" CP 2025 plateau dynamic\"\n\nLinks: [{\"title\":\"SLS-Enhanced Core-Boosted Linear Search for Anytime Maximum Satisfiability\",\"url\":\"https://drops.dagstuhl.de/entities/document/10.4230/LIPIcs.CP.2025.28\"},{\"title\":\"31st International Conference on Principles and Practice of Constraint Programming (CP 2025)\",\"url\":\"https://drops.dagstuhl.de/entities/volume/LIPIcs-volume-340\"},{\"title\":\"CP 2025 Accepted Papers\",\"url\":\"https://cp2025.a4cp.org/accepted_papers.html\"},{\"title\":\"Certified Core-Guided MaxSAT Solving | Springer Nature Link\",\"url\":\"https://link.springer.com/chapter/10.1007/978-3-031-38499-8_1\"},{\"title\":\"CP 2025\",\"url\":\"https://cp2025.a4cp.org/\"},{\"title\":\"CP: Principles and Practice of Constraint Programming 2027 2026 2025 ...\",\"url\":\"http://www.wikicfp.com/cfp/program?id=572\"},{\"title\":\"An Analysis of Core-Guided Maximum Satisfiability Solvers ...\",\"url\":\"https://drops.dagstuhl.de/storage/00lipics/lipics-vol271-sat2023/LIPIcs.SAT.2023.12/LIPIcs.SAT.2023.12.pdf\"},{\"title\":\"31st International Conference on Principles and Practice of ...\",\"url\":\"https://drops.dagstuhl.de/storage/00lipics/lipics-vol340-cp2025/LIPIcs.CP.2025.0/LIPIcs.CP.2025.0.pdf\"},{\"title\":\"Call for Papers - CP 2025\",\"url\":\"https://cp2025.a4cp.org/cfp.html\"},{\"title\":\"2025 Quarterly Report 3 | Association for Constraint ... - a4cp.org\",\"url\":\"https://www.a4cp.org/index.php/-97\"}]\n\nBased on the search results, I found information about the paper you're looking for:\n\nOle Lübke and Jeremias Berg presented \"SLS-Enhanced Core-Boosted Linear Search for Anytime Maximum Satisfiability\" at the 31st International Conference on Principles and Practice of Constraint Programming (CP 2025).\n\nThe paper addresses Maximum Satisfiability (MaxSAT), which involves minimizing a linear expression over Boolean variables subject to propositional clauses, with a focus on anytime MaxSAT solvers that compute low-cost solutions within limited computational time. The solvers discussed can be divided into SAT-based methods that use sophisticated reasoning and stochastic local search (SLS) methods that heuristically explore the search space.\n\nThe paper has been published in the Leibniz International Proceedings in Informatics (LIPIcs) series and is available at the Dagstuhl repository.\n\n\nREMINDER: You MUST include the sources above in your response to the user using markdown hyperlinks."}]},"uuid":"60317db8-4813-4867-93c9-92dfccf9888c","timestamp":"2026-05-09T23:52:06.591Z","sourceToolAssistantUUID":"1fb092c1-ea26-4f58-85b0-ed9b5182b272","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/home/zhuran24/claude-pj/zmd","sessionId":"7db7f276-a2bf-4762-b9b8-bb35f8cf3fb9","version":"2.1.138","gitBranch":"master","slug":"squishy-zooming-sphinx"}
{"parentUuid":"51649dab-cc44-453c-adbe-6f64e98bc346","isSidechain":true,"promptId":"6fa20b73-a995-4ae4-b928-344e193a8ce4","agentId":"aec9dfe82ab5889ef","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_0125zD5HzSNMzYQi8FSWxqfi","type":"tool_result","content":"Web search results for query: \"\"Practically Feasible Proof Logging\" \"Pseudo-Boolean Optimization\" CP 2025 LIPIcs authors\"\n\nLinks: [{\"title\":\"Practically Feasible Proof Logging for Pseudo-Boolean Optimization\",\"url\":\"https://drops.dagstuhl.de/storage/00lipics/lipics-vol340-cp2025/LIPIcs.CP.2025.21/LIPIcs.CP.2025.21.pdf\"},{\"title\":\"Practically feasible proof logging for pseudo-Boolean optimization | Lund University Publications\",\"url\":\"https://lup.lub.lu.se/search/publication/b5de3846-96d8-4689-874a-e0e3f939175c\"},{\"title\":\"31st International Conference on Principles and Practice of Constraint Programming (CP 2025)\",\"url\":\"https://drops.dagstuhl.de/entities/volume/LIPIcs-volume-340\"},{\"title\":\"Pseudo Boolean Competition 2025\",\"url\":\"http://www.cril.univ-artois.fr/PB25/\"},{\"title\":\"dblp: Jakob Nordström\",\"url\":\"https://dblp.org/pid/88/3143.html\"},{\"title\":\"Practically Feasible Proof Logging for Pseudo-Boolean Optimization - Andy Oertel\",\"url\":\"https://aoertel.de/publications/2025-practically-feasible-proof-logging-for-pb/\"},{\"title\":\"31st CP 2025: Glasgow, UK\",\"url\":\"https://dblp.org/db/conf/cp/cp2025.html\"},{\"title\":\"Practically Feasible Proof Logging for Pseudo-Boolean Optimization.\",\"url\":\"https://dblp.org/pid/02/358.xml\"},{\"title\":\"Pseudo-Boolean Reasoning About States and Transitions to Certify Dynamic Programming and Decision Diagram Algorithms\",\"url\":\"https://drops.dagstuhl.de/entities/document/10.4230/LIPIcs.CP.2024.9\"},{\"title\":\"Symmetric Core Learning for Pseudo-Boolean Optimization by ...\",\"url\":\"https://drops.dagstuhl.de/storage/00lipics/lipics-vol340-cp2025/LIPIcs.CP.2025.15/LIPIcs.CP.2025.15.pdf\"}]\n\nBased on the search results, here's the information about the paper \"Practically Feasible Proof Logging for Pseudo-Boolean Optimization\":\n\n## Publication Details\n\nThe paper was published in the 31st International Conference on Principles and Practice of Constraint Programming (CP 2025), in volume 340 of Leibniz International Proceedings in Informatics (LIPIcs), pages 21:1-21:27.\n\n## Authors\n\nThe authors are: Wietze Koops, Daniel Le Berre, Magnus O. Myreen, Jakob Nordström, Andy Oertel, Yong Kiam Tan, and Marc Vinyals.\n\n## Paper Overview\n\nThe paper addresses certifying solvers for combinatorial optimization, building on the success of proof logging and checking in Boolean satisfiability (SAT) solving. The work presents an efficient toolchain based on VeriPB and CakePB for formally verified pseudo-Boolean optimization, implementing proof logging for the full range of techniques in state-of-the-art solvers RoundingSat and Sat4j, including core-guided search and linear programming integration.\n\nThe experimental evaluation shows that proof logging and checking performance in this paradigm is now quite close to the level of SAT solving, and hence is clearly practically feasible.\n\n\nREMINDER: You MUST include the sources above in your response to the user using markdown hyperlinks."}]},"uuid":"424c9484-d44f-47d2-962c-6b7151a7c1a2","timestamp":"2026-05-09T23:52:07.006Z","sourceToolAssistantUUID":"51649dab-cc44-453c-adbe-6f64e98bc346","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/home/zhuran24/claude-pj/zmd","sessionId":"7db7f276-a2bf-4762-b9b8-bb35f8cf3fb9","version":"2.1.138","gitBranch":"master","slug":"squishy-zooming-sphinx"}
{"parentUuid":"acf40bbd-dbff-4897-abd6-2d91eddc8ed2","isSidechain":true,"promptId":"6fa20b73-a995-4ae4-b928-344e193a8ce4","agentId":"aec9dfe82ab5889ef","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01UR7PFF1WWjWFQrbaqvF7zR","type":"tool_result","content":"Web search results for query: \"cake_lpr 2026 SAT competition checker performance binary size improvement\"\n\nLinks: [{\"title\":\"GitHub - tanyongkiam/cake_lpr: LPR proof checking with CakeML\",\"url\":\"https://github.com/tanyongkiam/cake_lpr\"},{\"title\":\"Fast and Verified UNSAT Certificate Checking | Springer Nature Link\",\"url\":\"https://link.springer.com/chapter/10.1007/978-3-031-63498-7_26\"},{\"title\":\"cake_lpr: Verified Propagation Redundancy Checking in ...\",\"url\":\"https://cakeml.org/tacas21.pdf\"},{\"title\":\"cake_lpr: Veriﬁed Propagation Redundancy Checking in CakeML Yong Kiam Tan1(B)\",\"url\":\"https://link.springer.com/content/pdf/10.1007/978-3-030-72013-1_12.pdf\"},{\"title\":\"Verified Propagation Redundancy and Compositional UNSAT Checking in CakeML | International Journal on Software Tools for Technology Transfer\",\"url\":\"https://link.springer.com/article/10.1007/s10009-022-00690-y\"},{\"title\":\"cake_lpr: Verified Propagation Redundancy Checking in CakeML - PMC\",\"url\":\"https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7984575/\"},{\"title\":\"Verified LRAT and LPR Proof Checking with cake_lpr Yong Kiam Tan\",\"url\":\"https://satcompetition.github.io/2025/downloads/checkers/cakelpr.pdf\"},{\"title\":\"SAT Competition 2020 - ScienceDirect\",\"url\":\"https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S0004370221001235\"},{\"title\":\"SAT Competition\",\"url\":\"https://satcompetition.github.io/2023/tracks.html\"},{\"title\":\"cake_lpr: Verified Propagation Redundancy Checking in CakeML | Springer Nature Link\",\"url\":\"https://link.springer.com/chapter/10.1007/978-3-030-72013-1_12\"}]\n\nBased on the search results, here's what I found about cake_lpr performance improvements and binary size:\n\nAs of February 16, 2025, cake_lpr improved performance by around 5-10% and reduced its compiled binary size by around 20%.\n\nAdditionally, as of March 11, 2024, cake_lpr now natively supports LRAT/LPR proof files in binary format.\n\nRegarding its role in SAT competitions, cake_lpr was used to validate the unsatisfiability proofs in the 2020 SAT Competition because of its strong trust story combined with easy compilation and usage. The proof checker is formally verified down to the level of its x64 machine code implementation, which eliminates the possibility of bugs arising from compiler errors, code extraction, or other unverified additions to verified source code.\n\n\nREMINDER: You MUST include the sources above in your response to the user using markdown hyperlinks."}]},"uuid":"e3428a11-ba66-4816-8449-f6e84c5ef905","timestamp":"2026-05-09T23:52:10.101Z","sourceToolAssistantUUID":"acf40bbd-dbff-4897-abd6-2d91eddc8ed2","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/home/zhuran24/claude-pj/zmd","sessionId":"7db7f276-a2bf-4762-b9b8-bb35f8cf3fb9","version":"2.1.138","gitBranch":"master","slug":"squishy-zooming-sphinx"}
